From b7821b08cbf70895a8f6a499f17edf12d921a207 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 08:57:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] fix(operator): the boot check reads every Safe
 signer-migrate hands over -- the fleet identity Safe too

`jinn signer-migrate` hands over the service Safe and, when it is a different one, the fleet
identity Safe (the same agent EOA owns both). At boot the daemon checked the service Safe alone:
a keystore EOA that still owned the fleet identity Safe, or was an enabled module of it, went
unseen. A point the fixes of findings AK-2026-11-28-02 and -03 (the internal audit of 2026-11-28)
left open.

- signer/migrate.ts: migrationTargets, the Safes the migration hands over, in its order: the
  service Safe, then the fleet identity Safe with its own ERC-8004 identity when it is another
  Safe. The CLI verb builds its targets from it.
- main.ts: with `config.signer`, assertAgentEoaHasNoAuthority runs for each Safe of that list
  (the bootstrap result and the fleet state): the EOA as an owner, as an enabled module and as
  the owner of the agent NFT, as for the service Safe, and each Safe's other modules are named
  for review. Two parts of the service Safe's checks do not apply: the daemon builds no
  broadcaster on the fleet identity Safe, so the Kernel-is-owner check of that broadcaster has no
  counterpart (the CLI verbs that write through the identity Safe make it themselves, in
  openCliKernelSafeBroadcaster); and no threshold is read at boot for either Safe (the migration
  requires both to be 1/1).
- signer/attestation.ts: the check names the Safe it reads (`safeName`: `service Safe` by
  default, `fleet identity Safe`).
- tests: unit (migrationTargets: two Safes, one, a Safe without an identity; the refusal names the
  fleet identity Safe). Each rule was broken once on purpose and a test caught it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
